module
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Natural.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.SectionFive.CubeKernelEdges

public import Theory.Representation.NormalElementaryQuotient.SubgroupSpaceTransport

/-!
# Reverse-kernel line control in the three-pair branch

For a transposition t and an actor P in the same three-pair group, put
K = [C_M(t),P] on either permitted six-letter cover. If K has order greater
than two, then [M,P] is contained in K[M,t]. On the natural cover the total
movement has dimension at most two. On the augmentation cover the fixed
hyperplane's movement lies in the plane spanned by the other two pair-vectors;
large K fills that plane, and the transposition supplies the missing pair-vector.
The coordinate argument applies to every conjugate of the three-pair group.

The literal quotient action transports this comparison to the moved subgroup.
The incident central attachment contributes no commutators, so the comparison
holds for the entire vertex seed and its entire fixed kernel. At the last edge
of an overlapping critical path, take P generated by the starting and trapped
seeds. Its fixed-kernel commutator is exactly the retained reverse-kernel
obstruction. If that obstruction had order four, the edge-seed containments
would force the endpoint seed to normalize the trapped seed joined with the
starting seed, contradicting the selected failure of normalization.

The nonsylow consumer constructs the three-pair image and retains the same
cubic, neighbor conjugator, generating pair and every shared path vertex. Its
remaining nonzero obstruction has order exactly two. Excluding this line still
requires the source kernel argument; its order alone does not make it central
in a vertex stabilizer. MS93 (5.7) requires the strict slice index greater than
two, whereas the retained candidate-selection input only asserts escape from
the slice. The small-image shifts, strict slices, terminal contradictions,
original Sylow branches and distance-four estimates remain with this owner.
Neither largeDistance_knownSimple nor unconditional distance two is asserted.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, *Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two*
(1993), (3.3)(ii), (5.2)(ii)--(iii) and (5.7), printed pp.866--867,914--915,919.
All graph subgroups and maps belong to the original characteristic replacement;
both moved-cover orders sixteen and thirty-two are retained.
-/

public noncomputable section
open Subgroup Representation Representation.BinaryOffender BinaryPermutation
open Theory.SpecificGroups.SymmetricSix.BinaryFlags CoreCrossing
open CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.Offenders CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.Offenders.Six
